      "math_academy_id": "q-333626",
      "sequence_position": 9,
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has only one eigenvalue. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode.",
      "worked_solution": "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is $\\lambda =4$ and it's positive, we have a $\\text{unstable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14221258/assets/f67469d62d6a0eb61397927924e55868437ad09f405c3b2a9d73567af00c4620.png)",
